# .gitattributes — how git is allowed to touch the bytes in this repository.
#
# Rationale for every rule lives next to it. The long-form reasoning, the
# measurements behind the exceptions, and the checks that prove these rules did
# not rewrite history are in docs/ops/GIT-WORKFLOW.md.
#
# Last verified against the working tree: 2026-09-17.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Line endings
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Detect text and store it LF in history, so every checkout and every agent
# worktree sees the same bytes regardless of host or core.autocrlf setting.
# Verified 2026-09-17: the only tracked files containing CRLF are the eleven
# evidence CSVs excluded below, so switching this on renormalises nothing and
# `git status --porcelain` stays empty after adding this file.
* text=auto


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Evidence CSVs: preserved byte-for-byte, never renormalised
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# scripts/run_all.py, score.py, export.py, qa.py, enrich.py and
# search_fallback.py all write through `open(..., newline="")` + csv.writer,
# which emits RFC 4180 CRLF. Eleven tracked CSVs under data/ are already
# committed that way. `-text` disables conversion in both directions, so the
# bytes checked out equal the bytes committed on every platform.
#
# Measured 2026-09-17, because the obvious claim is wrong: plain `text=auto`
# does NOT renormalise these on its own. git skips the CRLF->LF clean filter
# when the blob already in the index contains CRLF, so `git status` stays empty
# with or without this line. What this line actually defends against is
# `git add --renormalize`, which bypasses that safety. With the rule commented
# out, `git add --renormalize -- data/` stages all eleven CSVs; with it in
# place it stages none. One careless renormalise would otherwise rewrite every
# data row in one commit and bury real row corrections under a whole-file diff,
# which AGENTS.md forbids ("Preserve evidence and corrections").
*.csv -text


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tracked binaries
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# `binary` is git's built-in macro for `-diff -merge -text`. Without it a
# three-way merge or an end-of-line filter can silently corrupt these files:
# a merged .docx or .xlsx is an unopenable zip, and Word/Excel will not say why.
# Marking them also keeps multi-megabyte blobs out of `git diff` output.
# Case matters: gitattributes patterns are case-sensitive on a case-sensitive
# filesystem, and logistics/ already tracks both IMG_4368.HEIC and
# IMG_4371.heic. Phone and camera imports produce upper-case extensions
# routinely, so every image and document type here lists its realistic
# spellings. git's own content sniffing limits the damage of a miss, but a
# sniffed file is not an explicitly protected one.
*.xlsx binary
*.docx binary
*.pptx binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.PDF  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.JPG  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.JPEG binary
*.heic binary
*.HEIC binary


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Readable diffs for the file types this repository is actually made of
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# git ships funcname drivers for these (git >= 2.36; this repo runs 2.43).
# They only change the hunk header, never the content: a markdown hunk is
# labelled with its nearest heading instead of `@@ -412,7 +412,9 @@`, which is
# the difference between a reviewable and an unreviewable diff in a 41,000-byte
# document like AGENTS.md.
*.md   diff=markdown
*.py   diff=python
*.html diff=html
*.css  diff=css


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated pipeline outputs: re-render, never hand-merge
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# These are tracked on purpose — they are the reviewable product of
# `node pipeline/cli.cjs render --all` — but they are never edited by hand.
#
# `text eol=lf` is load-bearing, not cosmetic: pipeline/render.cjs decides an
# output is stale with an exact string comparison
# (`fs.readFileSync(target, 'utf8') !== content`). On a host with
# core.autocrlf=true a plain `text=auto` checkout would hand every .md output
# back with CRLF and `make pipeline-check` would report every file stale
# forever. Pinning eol=lf makes the staleness check mean what it says.
#
# `-merge` selects git's binary merge driver: on a conflict git keeps the
# current side and flags it rather than writing conflict markers into a
# generated artifact. Conflict markers in a rendered deliverable are worse than
# a conflict, because the artifact still looks like a document. The correct
# resolution is always to re-render from the case file.
#
# `linguist-generated=true` collapses these by default in GitHub diffs, so a
# pull request shows the source change first and the render second.
pipeline/out/**      text eol=lf -merge linguist-generated=true
pipeline/trip/out/** text eol=lf -merge linguist-generated=true


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tracked SerpAPI response cache
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# .gitignore tracks this directory deliberately: every response cost monthly
# search quota and the JSON holds no key. It is a cache, not source. Merging
# two captures of the same query produces a document that was never returned by
# any API, so `-merge` forces a deliberate pick-a-side; `linguist-generated`
# keeps hundreds of cached responses out of the reviewable diff.
data/raw/serpapi.com/** -merge linguist-generated=true


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# npm lockfile
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# demo/carnegie-hall/package-lock.json is generated: 210 lines of resolved
# tarball URLs and integrity hashes accompanying a one-line package.json
# change. `linguist-generated=true` keeps it from dwarfing that change in the
# reviewable diff. `-merge` is the important half: git can merge two lockfiles
# textually into a dependency tree npm would never have resolved, and the
# result looks perfectly clean. The only correct resolution is to take one side
# and re-run `npm install`.
package-lock.json -merge linguist-generated=true


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The committed symlink
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# CLAUDE.md is stored with mode 120000 pointing at AGENTS.md, and AGENTS.md
# itself says "Preserve that link". The blob is the nine bytes `AGENTS.md`.
#
# `-text` guarantees no end-of-line filter is ever applied to those bytes,
# including on a host that materialises the link as a plain file.
# `-merge` stops a three-way merge from writing conflict markers into a
# one-line link target, which would leave a broken regular file where the
# symlink was.
#
# IMPORTANT, and the reason docs/ops/GIT-WORKFLOW.md carries a separate check:
# .gitattributes CANNOT defend the symlink against `core.symlinks=false`. That
# setting is resolved at checkout time, before attributes matter; the link is
# written as a plain file containing the target text, the index still records
# mode 120000, and `git status` reports a clean tree. No attribute can detect
# or prevent it. See the "core.symlinks hazard" section of the workflow doc for
# the check that does.
CLAUDE.md -text -merge
